  "step_2": "The verbal report (translated: 'Quite certain 3000 Euros are better than 50/50 6000 Euros, in my opinion') indicates that the individual values the high probability (90%) of getting 3000 Euros in Lottery A over the lower and more uncertain chance (45%) of getting 6000 Euros in Lottery B. The individual emphasizes the certainty or high likelihood of receiving 3000 Euros, suggesting a preference for security and aversion to risk. The justification centers on the reliability of the outcome in A, not on the distributional shape or skewness. There is no mention of the concentration of probability on unfavorable outcomes or any concept related to skewness.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on preferring more right-skewed lotteries (favoring B), but the individual clearly prefers A and justifies this by emphasizing the high probability of a positive outcome. The individual considers the likelihood and security of gains, which aligns more with risk aversion or expected utility reasoning rather than skewness-based preferences. The information considered by the individual (high chance of 3000) does not match the skewness-relevant information (probability concentration on low outcomes). Furthermore, the justification (favoring high probability of gain) contradicts the skewness reason, which would favor the lottery with more probability on bad outcomes (B). Therefore, there is no evidence the individual used skewness in their reasoning.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

{
  "step_1": "The decision reason involves assessing the skewness of each lottery, specifically whether a greater portion of the probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes (i.e., lower or zero payoffs) compared to favorable ones. In this context, both lotteries have only one nonzero outcome (favorable) and the rest is zero (unfavorable). Lottery A offers 3000 Euros with 0.2% probability (so 99.8% chance of 0), and Lottery B offers 6000 Euros with 0.1% probability (99.9% chance of 0). Thus, both lotteries are highly right-skewed because nearly all probability mass is on the unfavorable outcome (0 Euros). However, Lottery B assigns even less probability to the favorable outcome (0.1%) compared to Lottery A (0.2%), while offering a higher payoff. Therefore, Lottery B is more right-skewed than Lottery A. According to the decision reason, the more right-skewed lottery (B) would be preferred.",
  "step_2": "The verbal report states: 'Bei quasi der selben Chance vielleicht das doppelte Geld, gerne' (translated: 'With almost the same chance, maybe double the money, sure'). This indicates that the individual perceived the probabilities of winning as very similar (0.1% vs 0.2%) and focused on the potential payoff difference—6000 vs 3000 Euros. The justification centers on getting a chance at double the amount for a similar (albeit half as probable) chance. The individual appears to weigh the higher outcome more favorably despite the lower probability, suggesting a preference for higher payoff when probabilities are perceived as negligible or similar. No mention is made of skewness, distribution shape, or unfavorable vs favorable probability mass.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on preferring the more right-skewed lottery (i.e., the one with a smaller chance of winning, which is B). The individual’s reasoning, however, does not reference skewness or the concentration of probability on unfavorable outcomes. Instead, they justify their choice by comparing the payoffs ('double the money') under the perception that the chances are 'almost the same'. This suggests a simplification heuristic—ignoring small probability differences and focusing on outcome magnitude. While the individual might end up choosing B (which aligns with the skewness reason), their justification is outcome-focused and probability-minimizing, not distribution-shape-based. There is no evidence they considered the concept of skewness or preferred a lottery because it is more right-skewed. Therefore, the reasoning process does not align with the decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
