  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on assessing the overall shape of the distribution and specifically prefers more right-skewed lotteries (those with higher concentration of probability on unfavorable outcomes). Lottery A is more right-skewed than B, so the reason would choose A. However, the individual appears to prefer a 'higher chance of winning something,' which corresponds to a higher probability of a non-zero outcome. Lottery B has a 50% chance of a positive outcome (25% + 25%), while Lottery A has only 25%. So, if the individual acted on this reasoning, they would prefer B. This contradicts the skewness reason, which favors A. Moreover, the individual’s focus is on the chance of non-zero gain, not on the concentration of bad outcomes per se. The reasoning is related to skewness only in a superficial way (attention to low outcomes) but differs in direction and conceptual basis. The individual does not reference distributional shape or trade-offs in outcome probabilities beyond wanting to 'win something.' Thus, there is no evidence they used skewness as a rule. The justification moves in the opposite direction of the skewness reason and focuses on different information.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
}

